/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Pick S > 1.
   - That last row is the prize: S² more gates on a chip, each 1/S faster, for the same watts per mm².
   - If VDD stays fixed while the lengths shrink by S, the field rises by S.
   - The power a CMOS gate consumes has three parts:
   - The power-delay product PDP = Pavg × tp, the energy per switching event, compares logic families fairly: a gate that is slow because it saves power is not rewarded twice.
   - Once L is comparable to the source and drain depletion widths, the drain field reaches the source end of the channel and the gate no longer controls the channel alone.
   - Hot carriers: the lateral field peaks near the drain; some electrons gain enough energy to enter the oxide or break bonds at the interface, so VT drifts and gm falls over years.
   - NBTI (negative bias temperature instability): a pMOS held with its gate low at high temperature builds interface traps, so |VT| slowly rises and the gate slows; it partly recovers when the stress is removed.
*/
